  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides">Guides</a> › Preply vs italki</p>
  <h1>Preply vs italki: how to choose</h1>
  <p class="lede">Preply and italki are the two best-known marketplaces for 1-to-1 language lessons with a real tutor. They work in a similar way — you browse tutor profiles, choose a tutor, and take live video lessons — but they differ in how trials, pricing and lessons are organised. This guide gives you a simple framework for choosing between them, and a clear note: italki’s current prices, tutor numbers and rules are not verified here, so check italki directly before you pay.</p>

  <h2>How they are similar</h2>
  <p>Both Preply and italki are marketplaces, not schools: independent tutors set their own prices and teach 1-to-1 lessons online. On both, you can browse tutor profiles, read reviews, watch an introduction video and choose a tutor who fits your language, goal and budget. Both cover many languages, and both let you learn from anywhere, at times that suit you.</p>
  <p>That similarity is the point: the tutor matters more than the platform. A great tutor on either site will help you far more than a poor tutor on the other. So use this comparison to narrow your choice — then judge the individual tutors.</p>
  <h2>Tutor choice</h2>
  <p>On Preply, tutors are listed with their price per lesson, rating, number of students and lessons taught, and specialities such as business English or exam preparation. Some tutors carry a “Super Tutor” badge. On italki, tutors are generally listed as professional teachers or community tutors, which is a useful distinction: professional teachers usually have qualifications and structured lessons, while community tutors usually offer cheaper conversation practice.</p>
  <p>When you compare, look at the same things on both sites: the tutor’s introduction, their reviews, how many lessons they have taught, and whether their speciality matches your goal. Do not choose on price alone.</p>
  <h2>Trial lessons</h2>
  <p>Preply’s trial is a paid lesson of 25 or 50 minutes, and if the tutor is not a good fit, Preply offers a free replacement trial with a different tutor. italki also offers trial lessons, usually shorter and cheaper than a full lesson, so you can test a tutor before committing. The details — length, price and replacement rules — change over time, so check the current trial terms on each site before you book.</p>
  <p>Whichever site you use, take the trial seriously: prepare what you want to say, notice how the tutor explains things, and ask yourself whether you would enjoy learning with this person every week.</p>
  <h2>Pricing and how you pay</h2>
  <p>Preply works on a subscription: after the trial, you pay for a set of lessons that renews every 28 days. Tutors set their own per-lesson prices. italki generally works differently: you usually buy lesson credits and book lessons one at a time, without a subscription. That difference matters — a subscription suits learners who want a fixed routine, while pay-as-you-go suits learners who want flexibility.</p>
  <p>Prices on both sites vary widely by tutor, language and experience. Do not compare the cheapest tutor on one site with the most expensive on the other — compare tutors at a similar level, for your language, and check what is included in the price.</p>
  <h2>Lesson style and flexibility</h2>
  <p>Preply lessons take place in Preply’s own online classroom, with a whiteboard and shared materials, and lessons can be rescheduled up to a set time before the lesson. italki lessons usually take place on a video call, and the tutor often uses their own materials. Both approaches work — the classroom is more structured, while a video call is more flexible.</p>
  <p>Also check the practical rules: how far in advance you can reschedule, what happens if you miss a lesson, and how easy it is to change tutor. These small rules matter more than they seem once you are learning every week.</p>
  <h2>How to decide in five minutes</h2>
  <p>Answer these questions. Do you want a fixed routine or full flexibility? If routine, Preply’s subscription may suit you; if flexibility, italki’s pay-as-you-go may suit you better. Do you want a qualified teacher or affordable conversation practice? Both sites offer both, but italki labels the difference more clearly. And which site has the better tutor for your exact goal — business English, exam preparation, or conversation — at a price you can afford?</p>
  <p>Then take a trial lesson on the site that looks better, and judge the tutor, not the brand. If the trial goes well, stay. If not, try a tutor on the other site. The best platform is the one with the tutor you actually enjoy learning with.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Comparing the cheapest tutor on one site with the most expensive on the other.</li>
    <li>Choosing on brand name alone, without looking at the individual tutor.</li>
    <li>Skipping the trial and buying a big package first.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the rescheduling and cancellation rules until you need them.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Compare tutors at a similar level, for your language, on both sites.</li>
    <li>Take a trial lesson before you commit to anything.</li>
    <li>Check the current trial, pricing and rescheduling rules on each site — they change.</li>
    <li>Judge the tutor, not the brand: a great tutor on either site beats a poor tutor on the other.</li>
  </ul>
